;; The interval differential corpus: every KNOWN divergence from a
;; real PostgreSQL, one entry per sample. Generated by
;;   (datahike.fuzz.differential/run-surface :interval 0 0)
;;
;; A divergence NOT listed here fails `bb fuzz interval`. A listed
;; one that now AGREES also fails, with BASELINE DRIFT -- so a fix
;; is not complete until its entries are pruned.
;;
;; THIS IS THE STARTING STATE, and it is almost everything: an
;; interval is stored as the user's own text today and
;; `cast-scalar` has no interval arm at all, so `'garbage'::interval`
;; returns `garbage` and `'10 days' < '9 days'` is string
;; comparison on `'1' < '9'`.
;;
;; Reasons, and which step of Phase 2 closes each:
;;   PARSER      -- DecodeInterval / DecodeISO8601Interval /
;;                  deltatktbl. Steps 2-3.
;;   PARSER+OUT  -- also needs EncodeInterval. Step 4.
;;   CARRIER     -- equality, hashing and ordering, which must come
;;                  from the carrier's own .equals/.hashCode/
;;                  compareTo because GROUP BY and DISTINCT are
;;                  datahike's and take no comparator. Step 4.
;;   ARITHMETIC  -- the operator functions. Step 5.
;;
;; Six projections per literal, because the datetime corpus's one
;; (`::text`) cannot tell a parser loss from a renderer loss and
;; never reaches a column, an array, a comparison or GROUP BY --
;; three real divergences hid in exactly those paths. Here each
;; literal is projected through ::text, justify_interval (the
;; canonical VALUE), a column round-trip, an array element,
;; equality against a reference, and the bare cast.
;;
;; Baseline 2026-10-04: 835 of 1043 samples diverge.
;; By reason: {"ARITHMETIC" 7, "CARRIER" 31, "PARSER" 775, "PARSER+CARRIER" 7, "PARSER+OUT" 15}

